From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Gardner <jamestexasgardner@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 20:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] devices/vsock: with a port map, a TSI datagram socket
 carries replies only

A configured port map is the complete list of guest listeners the host
may reach (the stream half is the previous patch). The datagram proxy
never consulted it. Its listen is a no-op, so a guest UDP bind to a
fixed port is not published, but a guest sendto makes the proxy bind a
host UDP socket of its own at the unspecified address and ephemeral
port, and every datagram that arrives on it was forwarded to the guest,
from any source, on every host interface. One sendto, even one the
VMM's sandbox then refused to transmit, turned the guest socket into a
listener the map does not list.

With a map configured, the proxy now remembers the peers the guest
named (each sendto destination and a connect peer, IP and port, the 64
most recent) and forwards only datagrams whose source is one of them;
any other datagram is read and dropped. This is the rule the kernel
already applies to a connected UDP socket, extended to sendto. Without
a map, every datagram is forwarded as before, and Unix-domain datagram
sockets are unaffected either way: the map lists INET ports.

Signed-off-by: James Gardner <jamestexasgardner@gmail.com>
---
